  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1208 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nModels in good agreement through the extended period, with a few\ndifferences for mid to late next week.\n\nSaturday...a quick moving upper shortwave is forecast to track\nacross Colorado on Saturday. This will bring a mix of weather to\nsouthern Colorado through Saturday night.\n\nFor the Continental Divide, snow showers are forecast Saturday\nand Saturday evening. A Winter Weather Advisory is in effect for\nthe Central Mountains, where 3 to 7 inches of snow are possible,\nalong with 55 mph wind gusts, with the highest accumulations\nnorth of Cottonwood Pass. This shower activity will peak\nSaturday afternoon, and dissipate through the overnight hours.\n\nConditions on the Palmer Divide could be briefly interesting. A\nvery strong cold front is forecast to drop south by late\nmorning. A warm start is expected, with temperatures falling\nquickly with the frontal passage, down into the lower to mid\n30s. Very strong winds, 45 to 55 mph along with a band of rain\nchanging to snow is expected with the frontal passage. There\ncould be a period during the afternoon with brief heavy snow\nbursts and blowing snow conditions. Overall, snow amounts will\nbe minor, less than an inch, but travel impacts due to poor\nvisibility and slick road could cause problems. These conditions\nwill need to be monitored for appropriate highlights, whether\nits short fused snow squall warnings or an advisory.  \n\nFor the rest of the Plains, the cold front will rapidly drop\nsouth, clearing into New Mexico by early afternoon. This is\nsignificantly faster than previous model runs, which had it\npassing through by late afternoon. This may impact afternoon\nhigh temperatures, which may occur early, with highs along and\nsouth of Highway 50 in the lower 60s. Temperatures will rapidly\nfall behind the cold front, falling into the 40s by early\nevening. The main concern will be very strong winds. High Wind\nWatches are in place for portions of the Plains, where wind\ngusts could exceed 60 mph during the afternoon and early\nevening. Recent dry conditions could lead to areas of blowing\ndust, and strong cross winds on west-east highways will likely\npose hazardous travel conditions. Model guidance continues to\ndevelop a narrow band of showers with the front as it pushes\nsouth. Given the earlier frontal passage, warmer temperatures\nwill likely keep precip type as rain, with a few snow flakes\npossible near the Eastern Mountains and Raton Ridge. With the\ncurrent model guidance, the main impacts will be from wind.\n\nSunday into Tuesday...flat upper ridging is forecast to move\nacross Colorado for early next week. This will lead to daily\nbreezy conditions in wind prone areas along the I-25 corridor.\nThere could be a few snow showers along the Continental Divide\nwith any weak embedded waves that move through the upper flow.\nOverall, temperatures will warm back into the 50s and 60s each\nafternoon.\n\nWednesday into Friday...the next upper storm system is forecast\nto begin impacting the region Wednesday, with widespread\nprecipitation possible Thursday into Friday. All guidance has an\nupper system tracking across the region, with the main\ndifferences being storm track. The GFS is furthest north, while\nthe ECMWF, Canadian and various ensemble and AI guidance are\nsouth. At this time, mountains look to see snowfall, with\naccumulations expected. On the Plains, the guidance with the\nsouthern tracks wrap a trowal south along the I-25 corridor with\npossibly our first snow of the winter season. Ensemble and\ndeterministic guidance trends have shown some consistency over\nthe past few model cycles.",
